# Architecture

## Loader boundary
- Only `com.davisr.thedreamtable.fabric` may reference loader APIs (`net.fabricmc`, and later `net.neoforged`/`net.minecraftforge`), and no other package may reference `fabric`. `verifyNoLoaderImports` (run by `check`/`build`) enforces this.
- Everything else reaches the loader through `platform/` seams via `Platform`: `paths()`, `blockBreaks()`, `network()`, `serverEvents()`, `menus()`, `content()`, and on the client only `clientNetwork()`, `clientEvents()`, `clientRendering()`. Add a seam method instead of calling a loader API.
- `fabric/TheDreamTableFabric` and `TheDreamTableFabricClient` are the entrypoints: they install the platform first, then call `TheDreamTableMod.init()` / `TheDreamTableClient.init()`. Nothing may touch `Platform` in static initializers that run before install.
- Server-safe seams (`Platform`, `PlatformPaths`, `BlockBreakGuard`, `ServerNetwork`, `ClientNetwork`, `ServerEvents`, `Menus`, `ExtendedMenuProvider`, `Content`) never reference `net.minecraft.client`; client seam implementations are created only in `FabricPlatform.installClient()`, so a dedicated server never loads them.
- C2S handlers given to `ServerNetwork.registerClientToServer` run on the server thread with the authenticated player; they still revalidate everything as described under "Server owns the state".
- Tables open through `Platform.menus().open(player, table)`; the opening data is `DreamTableBlockEntity.OpenData` written by the menu type's codec.

- `block/entity`: `DreamTableBlockEntity` keeps one `TableSession` per player (a map by UUID), ticks them, saves them, and sends each to its owner. A session holds everything a player does at the table: page, chosen farm, placement. It delegates to `MaterialDesk` (slots + `MaterialLedger`: delivered and used counts, Creative counts, swaps), `SessionBuild` (begin, pace, tick, stops, finish; reach it with `session.build()`), `BuildProgress` (phase, step, credit, owner, pace, placed and paid steps, the current `BuildIssue`, repair flag and kept count), `SessionAreas` (problem checks, area claims, finished farms) and `FarmPlacement` (anchor and facing).
- Sync: chunk data carries no sessions (`ClientView` marker). `TableSession.sync()` marks the session dirty; the table sends `SessionSyncPayload` with `writeForClient()` to the owner at most once per tick. A client without its session asks with `RequestSessionPayload` (`OwnSessions`). Data a client draws must be added to `writeForClient`.
- My Schematics on a dedicated server: opening a table sends `ServerSchematicsPayload` (the server's list); a client not running the server switches `FarmTemplates` to remote mode and fetches each farm through `TemplateCodec` when `get` misses (`client/ServerSchematics`). Singleplayer shares the same static lists and ignores these payloads.
- A built-in farm id with no template is retired: its session is discarded with the items returned. Finished farms don't depend on it: `FarmAreas` saves each one's design (`Layout`, via `TemplateCodec`) under its persisted, globally unique `LayoutId` from `FarmTemplates.newSavedId` and registers it with `keepSaved` on load (all worlds load on `SERVER_STARTED`). The unique suffix prevents layouts with the same legacy signature but different block-entity NBT from replacing one another. Repair, Take Down, missing blocks and storage use that layout; `FarmOptions.byId` maps saved ids to their farm. My Farms offers only Forget when a farm has no layout (finished before 2.0.0-beta by a design that has since changed). On servers, clients fetch saved designs like imported ones.
- Server settings live in `DreamTableConfig` (`config/the_dream_table.properties`), client preferences in `client/ClientSettings`.
- Imported schematics are parsed through `ImportedStructures`' cache (by file time and size), and `FarmTemplates` swaps in the whole new list at once. Don't fill shared lists in place.
- Never store per-player state on the table itself, and never read another player's session. Server code gets the session from the acting player; client code uses `table.session(localPlayer)`. Never cache a session on the client: each `SessionSyncPayload` replaces the client's copy.
- The farm code (`FarmBuilder`, `FarmDismantler`, `FarmSpawns`) takes a `TableSession`, never the table.
- `farm`:
  - `FarmBuilder` places one step per call.
  - `FarmDismantler` takes an unfinished build apart on discard.
  - `ObstacleRules` decides what may be cleared.
  - `FarmSpawns` handles mobs and buckets.
  - `MaterialSwaps` lists compatible blocks.
- `farm/schematic`: one reader per format (`VanillaReader`, `SpongeReader`, `LitematicaReader`), driven by `SchematicReader`, which returns a `Result`: the template and its `ImportNote` warnings, or no template and the rejecting note. Readers resolve each palette once, report blocks this game lacks with `StepCollector.unknown`, and attach block data through the collector's position map (never by scanning steps). Files over `MAX_BYTES` (64 MB) are rejected before parsing; `NbtSizeTracker` enforces it while reading.
- `ImportedStructures.Imports` carries warnings by id and `Rejected` files; `FarmTemplates.notes`/`rejected` serve them locally or from the server (`ServerSchematicsPayload` sends both). `FarmTemplates.reloadImports` runs at most once per second for the same world.
- `TemplateCodec.encode(template, true)` keeps block entity data; use it for anything saved in the world (finished farms' `Layout`). Network copies leave it out.
- `farm/legacy`: code-built farms, used only when a bundled `.schem` can't be read.
- `screen`: `DreamTableScreen` only wires things together. `TableButtons` is the server side of ordinary synchronized menu buttons. My Farms care actions use `FarmCarePayload`, because their stable string keys cannot fit a screen button id. Page and ordinary button ids are constants in `DreamTableScreenHandler`.
- `screen/page`: one `TablePage` per menu page. `ProjectState` reads the synced phase on the client.
- `screen/ui`: `Theme`, `Painter` (cards, buttons, small `chip` buttons for filters), `Viewport`, `SearchBox` (shown on the swap page and My Farms; kept when going from a farm's page back to My Farms).
- A finished farm's public identity is `FarmKey(dimension, instanceId)`, serialized as `dimension|UUID`. `instanceId` is immutable and independent of table/position; positional ids exist only for legacy migration and internal area release. Its validated custom name, accepted grants, and pending invites belong to the finished record and survive save/load and Repair. Per-player favorites, tracking, and acknowledgement cursors live separately in overworld `FarmPreferences`; temporary sharing disablement must not prune them. Resolve public keys through `FarmDirectory`, and authorize every action through `FarmAccess` using the authenticated player UUID and server-owned `FarmRecord`.
- Persisted collection limits must be enforced before accepting mutations. Writers preserve all accepted state; readers remain bounded and reject malformed external NBT.
- My Farms filtering and sorting live in `farm/FarmListing` (pure, tested); the client keeps the chosen filter, sort and dimension in `LoadedFarmsView`. Filtered lists contain places in the latest client snapshot only for rendering and navigation; server mutations always use the public `FarmLoader.Entry.key` through `FarmCarePayload`. `FarmLoader.Entry.storage` is the storage use in percent, or `UNKNOWN`.
- `client/FarmFaces`: caches each farm's quads, tints and the step covering each face, per template, signature, swaps and facing; cleared on resource reload. Never look up block models per frame: draw from `FarmFaces`.
- `client/FarmMiniature`: the block entity renderer that floats a small model of the local player's chosen farm above the table, from `FarmFaces.outside`, within 32 blocks.
- `client/clipboard` and `client/hologram`: the world-space board and the ghost blocks. While placing, the hologram shows the whole farm; while building, only up to `LAYERS_AHEAD` layers above the next block, unless the player pressed H (`DreamTableHologram.toggleLayers`). `client/OtherFarms` draws other players' areas from `FarmAreasPayload` when Show Other Farms is on.

## Server owns the state
The client only previews with `handler.preview(...)` and sends either an ordinary button id or a purpose-built payload. `TableButtons` and authenticated payload handlers change the session, and the session calls `sync()`. Payload handlers revalidate the open table, acting player and stable resource key; never trust a client list index.

## Adding a built-in farm
The Dream Table ships the essential vanilla farms, one design each, made or chosen by Davi; bigger or other versions are for My Schematics. Never generate or invent farm designs: add the `.schem` Davi sends to `data/the_dream_table/structures/`, a `FarmOption` to `FarmOptions.ALL` and to exactly one `FarmOptions.Category` (a test checks this), register the file in `FarmTemplates`, and add it to `BuiltInFarmsTest`. Category pages are `PAGE_CATEGORY + index` and open with `BUTTON_CATEGORY + index`; farm buttons stay the index in `ALL`.

## Adding a menu page
1. Add `PAGE_*` and `BUTTON_*` constants in `DreamTableScreenHandler`.
2. Handle the buttons in `TableButtons`.
3. Create a `TablePage` in `screen/page` that draws through `PageContext` (`card`, `button`, `go`).
4. Register the page in `DreamTableScreen.page()`.
